L=[]
L.append(dict(cid="K02",title="Random 2-complexes are not simply connected at density c/sqrt(n)",grade="B",importance=7,novelty=4,value=6,named=False,
named_reason="The sharp-constant statement is Luria-Newman Conjecture 1.3 (person-named authors' conjecture); this order-of-magnitude lower bound is the unnamed weaker part, explicitly flagged as open in their Discussion.",
W="Take the complete graph on n vertices and add each of its triangles independently with probability p (the Linial-Meshulam random 2-complex). It is known to be simply connected once p is a bit above (9.48 n)^(-1/2), and to have a nontrivial (hyperbolic) fundamental group when p is at most n^(-1/2-eps). Is the threshold really of order n^(-1/2)? Show that for some small constant c > 0, at p = c/sqrt(n) there is with high probability a loop that cannot be contracted.",
M=r"Let $Y\sim Y_2(n,p)$: complete 1-skeleton on $[n]$, each 2-face included independently with probability $p$. Then $\pi_1(Y)\cong\langle g_{ij}\ (1\le i<j\le n)\mid g_{1j}=1\ (2\le j\le n);\ g_{ij}g_{jk}=g_{ik}\ \text{for each 2-face }\{i<j<k\}\in Y\rangle$. Prove: there exists $c>0$ such that $\Pr[\pi_1(Y_2(n,c\,n^{-1/2}))\ne 1]\to1$ as $n\to\infty$. (Known: nontrivial a.a.s. for $p\le n^{-1/2-\varepsilon}$ [BHK]; trivial a.a.s. for $p\ge c\,n^{-1/2}$ with $c>(27/256)^{1/2}\approx0.325$ [Luria--Newman].)",
why="Simple connectivity is the 2-dimensional analogue of Erdos-Renyi connectivity, and in Y_2(n,p) the fundamental group survives far beyond the vanishing of homology (around 2 log n / n), so it is controlled by non-abelian, hyperbolic-type obstructions. Upper and lower bounds currently differ by a factor n^eps; Luria and Newman explicitly say any Omega(1/sqrt n) lower bound would be of great interest. Closing it requires quantitative control of random group presentations right up to the collapse window, in the spirit of the sharp collapse threshold for random triangular groups.",
issues="Verified: Luria-Newman (arXiv:1806.03351, DCG 2021) Discussion states 'any lower bound of p = Omega(1/sqrt n) for the threshold probability for simple connectivity would be of great interest'; BHK (JAMS 2011) lower bound is p <= n^{-1/2-eps}. Extended searches (2022-2026) found no improvement. No OpenAI title on random complexes/fundamental groups. Not a board duplicate. M is correct (edge-path group with star spanning tree at vertex 1). Constant: gamma=4^4/3^3=256/27, so (gamma n)^{-1/2} ~ 0.325 n^{-1/2}. No attack succeeded: at p=c/sqrt n every edge lies in ~c sqrt n triangles, so there is no local obstruction; nontriviality must be global. Slightly narrow (random topology), hence B.",
refs=["https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.03351","https://arxiv.org/abs/0711.2704","https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.7165"]))
L.append(dict(cid="K08",title="Inducibility of the 4-vertex path",grade="B",importance=7,novelty=3,value=7,named=False,
named_reason="Inducibility is a parameter (Pippenger-Golumbic); the P4 case is not a person-named conjecture.",
W="Pick 4 vertices at random from a large graph; how likely is it that they induce exactly a path a-b-c-d (with no other edges among them)? The inducibility of P4 is the largest this probability can be in the limit. For every other graph on 4 vertices the answer is known, but for P4 it is only known to lie between about 0.2014 and 0.2033. Determine it.",
M=r"For a graph $G$ on $n\ge4$ vertices let $d(G)$ be the fraction of 4-subsets of $V(G)$ that induce a copy of $P_4$, and let $\lambda_{P_4}=\lim_{n\to\infty}\max_{|G|=n} d(G)$ (the maxima are non-increasing in $n$, so the limit exists). Known: $1173/5824=0.20140\ldots\le\lambda_{P_4}\le 0.20332375$. Determine $\lambda_{P_4}$; in particular decide whether $\lambda_{P_4}=1173/5824$.",
why="Inducibility is a central extremal parameter, and P4 is the smallest graph whose inducibility is unknown: every other graph on at most 4 vertices is settled. P4 is self-complementary and the best construction (Even-Zohar-Linial) is an intricate iterated composition rather than a simple blow-up, and large flag-algebra computations still leave a gap. Determining the value would require either a matching certificate plus stability analysis or a genuinely new type of extremal construction.",
issues="Upper bound updated: Lidicky-Volec, arXiv:2610.33850 (Oct 2026, 9-vertex flag algebra exploiting self-complementarity) prove I(P4) <= 0.20332375, improving Vaughan's ~0.204513 used by the candidate; W and M corrected. Bodnar-Pikhurko (EJC 33(2) P39, 2026) confirm P4 is the only unresolved 4-vertex graph. No OpenAI title on inducibility. Not a board duplicate. Risk: further SDP computations could close the gap with the existing construction (making the resolution computational rather than conceptual); the shrinking gap (now ~0.0019) makes this more plausible.",
refs=["https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.1205","https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v33i2p39/pdf/","https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33850","https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.01596"]))
L.append(dict(cid="K14",title="Ehrhart positivity of the Birkhoff polytope",grade="B",importance=7,novelty=3,value=7,named=False,
named_reason="Borderline: Liu's survey attributes the conjecture to a talk of Stanley, but it is usually stated as 'Birkhoff polytopes are Ehrhart positive', not as 'Stanley's conjecture'; the 2026 paper titled 'Stanley's conjecture' concerns a different (leading-coefficient asymptotic) statement. Owner may choose to treat it as person-named.",
W="Let H_n(t) be the number of n x n matrices of nonnegative integers whose rows and columns all sum to t (semi-magic squares). For fixed n, H_n(t) is a polynomial in t of degree (n-1)^2. Are all of its coefficients positive, for every n?",
M=r"Let $H_n(t)=\#\{A\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}^{n\times n}:\ A\mathbf 1=A^{T}\mathbf 1=t\mathbf 1\}$, the Ehrhart polynomial of the Birkhoff polytope $B_n$. Prove or disprove: for every $n\ge1$, every coefficient of $t^i$ in $H_n(t)$, $0\le i\le (n-1)^2$, is strictly positive.",
why="The Birkhoff polytope is among the most studied polytopes in combinatorics, yet its Ehrhart polynomial is known only for n <= 10 and its volume has no closed form. Ehrhart positivity is a central theme (Liu's survey), and B_n is the most prominent polytope for which it is conjectured but unproved; positivity would follow from all roots having negative real part, observed for small n. A proof would likely need a new structural formula for the coefficients, e.g. via the recent leading-coefficient asymptotics.",
issues="Verified by my own computation: H_3 = 1 + 9/4 t + 15/8 t^2 + 3/4 t^3 + 1/8 t^4 and all 10 coefficients of H_4 positive (DP count + interpolation). Liu survey (arXiv:1711.09962, Sec. 5) lists it as open, citing roots with negative real part for the first nine polynomials. arXiv:2611.25973 (Nov 2026, unrefereed) proves only c(n,k)/c(n,0) ~ n^{3k}/(2^k k!) for fixed k, which gives positivity of the top few coefficients for large n only. No OpenAI title on Birkhoff/Ehrhart. Not a board duplicate (board has a 3x3 magic square of squares problem, unrelated). Low novelty (well-known conjecture). Naming flagged above.",
refs=["https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.09962","https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0202267","https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.25973","https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.10852"]))
L.append(dict(cid="K20",title="Unbounded partial quotients of the cube root of 2",grade="A",importance=9,novelty=2,value=8,named=False,
named_reason="Classical folklore question (Khintchine, Lang-Trotter); not called anyone's conjecture.",
W="The continued fraction of the square root of 2 is periodic, but no pattern is known in the continued fraction of the cube root of 2 = [1; 3, 1, 5, 1, 1, 4, 1, 1, 8, 1, 14, ...]. It is believed to behave like a random number and have arbitrarily large terms, but it has never been proved that its terms are unbounded. Prove that they are.",
M=r"Let $2^{1/3}=[a_0;a_1,a_2,\ldots]$ be the simple continued fraction. Prove $\sup_n a_n=\infty$; equivalently, $2^{1/3}$ is not badly approximable: $\liminf_{q\to\infty} q\,\|q\,2^{1/3}\|=0$, where $\|\cdot\|$ is the distance to the nearest integer.",
why="Not a single real algebraic number of degree at least 3 is known to have bounded or unbounded partial quotients, although all numerical evidence follows the Gauss-Kuzmin statistics of a random real. Roth's theorem gives |q alpha - p| >> q^{-1-eps} and is silent on this question. 2^{1/3}, whose field has a complex embedding, is the simplest test case; it connects Diophantine approximation with the homogeneous dynamics of diagonal orbits.",
issues="Open and classical. Haynes, arXiv:2310.12703 (v1 only, Oct 2023, no journal version found) claims that all totally real cubic irrationals are well approximable via Lindenstrauss-Weiss/Ratner; this is unverified and in any case excludes Q(2^{1/3}), which has a complex embedding. Flag: if Haynes is correct the 'first algebraic example' novelty is gone, but 2^{1/3} remains open. arXiv:2610.07471 (Oct 2026) uses (2^{1/3},4^{1/3}) only as an example for a gap theorem; it does not address partial quotients. No OpenAI title on continued fractions or algebraic approximation. Board has 'Continued fraction of the P-constant' and prime continued fractions (different). I softened the candidate's 'natural frontier' framing, which relied on the unrefereed claim. Very hard (low tractability), but central and cleanly stated.",
refs=["https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.12703","https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.16596","https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.07471","https://www.ams.org/journals/mcom/1955-09-049/S0025-5718-1955-0068900-3"]))
L.append(dict(cid="K26",title="Randomized query complexity of monotone graph properties beyond n^{4/3}",grade="A",importance=8,novelty=4,value=7,named=False,
named_reason="Unnamed polynomial-improvement sub-question of the randomized Aanderaa-Karp-Rosenberg (Yao/Karp) conjecture, which is named; the full Omega(n^2) conjecture would be rejected.",
W="A graph property on n vertices is monotone if adding edges never destroys it (like connectivity or containing a triangle). A randomized algorithm may ask whether individual edges are present and must answer correctly with probability at least 2/3. Every nontrivial monotone property is believed to need about n^2 queries, but since Hajnal (1991) and Chakrabarti-Khot (2001) the best general lower bound has been stuck at roughly n^{4/3}. Can the exponent 4/3 be improved?",
M=r"Let $E_n=\binom{[n]}{2}$. A graph property is $f:\{0,1\}^{E_n}\to\{0,1\}$ invariant under the induced action of $S_n$; it is monotone if $G\subseteq H\Rightarrow f(G)\le f(H)$ and nontrivial if non-constant. $R(f)$ is the least $d$ such that some probability distribution on deterministic decision trees of depth $\le d$ querying variables in $E_n$ satisfies $\Pr_T[T(G)=f(G)]\ge 2/3$ for every $G$. Prove: there exist $\varepsilon>0$, $c>0$ such that $R(f)\ge c\,n^{4/3+\varepsilon}$ for every $n\ge2$ and every nontrivial monotone graph property $f$ on $n$ vertices.",
why="Randomized evasiveness of graph properties is one of the oldest problems in query complexity; the deterministic (Rivest-Vuillemin, Kahn-Saks-Sturtevant) and quantum (Omega(n), 2021) analogues are settled up to constants, while the randomized exponent has been stuck at 4/3 since 1991. The 4/3 barrier comes from combining graph packing with influence/critical-probability arguments, and breaking it would require a genuinely new handle on randomized algorithms for symmetric monotone functions.",
issues="Current best lower bound Omega(n^{4/3} log^{1/3} n) (Chakrabarti-Khot, ICALP 2001 / RSA 2007), confirmed as best known; extended searches for 2023-2026 found nothing better. OpenAI title 'A Sharp Threshold Bound for Monotone Graph Properties' (2026-09-25): only the title is known. It reads as a threshold-width (Friedgut-Kalai/Bourgain-Kalai) result, not a query lower bound, but threshold arguments do feed into Hajnal-type bounds, so flag it and check it before posting. Small fix: 'finitely supported' is unnecessary (there are finitely many trees). The question is natural and well-posed; trivial bound Omega(n) holds via D<=O(R^2) for monotone f.",
refs=["https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/rsa.20164","https://arxiv.org/abs/cs/0508071","https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.12629","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evasiveness"]))
L.append(dict(cid="K32",title="A flat cyclotomic polynomial with five odd prime factors",grade="B",importance=6,novelty=4,value=7,named=False,
named_reason="Question stated by Kaplan (2010) but not called 'Kaplan's conjecture/problem'.",
W="Call the cyclotomic polynomial Phi_n flat if every coefficient is -1, 0 or 1. Flat examples are known when n has up to four distinct odd prime factors (for example Phi_{3*5*31*929}). Is there a flat Phi_n where n has five distinct odd prime factors? None has ever been found, and no reason is known why they cannot exist.",
M=r"Let $\omega_{\mathrm{odd}}(n)$ be the number of distinct odd primes dividing $n$. Does there exist $n$ with $\omega_{\mathrm{odd}}(n)=5$ such that every coefficient of $\Phi_n(x)\in\mathbb Z[x]$ lies in $\{-1,0,1\}$? (Since $\Phi_{2m}(x)=\pm\Phi_m(-x)$ for odd $m>1$ and $\Phi_{n}(x)=\Phi_{\mathrm{rad}(n)}(x^{n/\mathrm{rad}(n)})$, one may assume $n=p_1p_2p_3p_4p_5$ with distinct odd primes.)",
why="Flatness is the extreme case of the long-studied question of how large cyclotomic coefficients can be. Order 3 has infinitely many flat examples and structural theorems, and order 4 has Kaplan's infinite family, but at order 5 there is not a single example, even though no obstruction is known. Either a construction or a non-existence proof would need a new understanding of how the inclusion-exclusion cancellation can survive five primes.",
issues="Confirmed open: Kaplan, Integers 10 (2010) A30 states it; Bachman, arXiv:2602.07727 (2026): 'Despite extensive computations, no flat polynomials of order 5 or higher have ever been found.' JMM abstract (Arnold-type attempts) gives no resolution. No OpenAI title (grep cyclotomic/flat: unrelated). Not a board duplicate. Fixed the reduction remark in M: Phi_n coefficients depend only on the odd squarefree kernel (with x -> -x for the factor 2). The answer could in principle come from a lucky search, but candidate n are enormous. A natural, striking existence question, though a narrow subfield.",
refs=["https://emis.univie.ac.at/journals/INTEGERS/papers/k30/k30.pdf","https://arxiv.org/abs/1207.5811","https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.07727","https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.04034"]))
L.append(dict(cid="K38",title="Reachability for two-interval piecewise affine maps",grade="B",importance=7,novelty=4,value=7,named=False,
named_reason="Longstanding open problem in reachability/verification, not attributed as anyone's conjecture.",
W="Fix a rational cut point c in (0,1) and two affine maps with rational coefficients, and let f use the first map on [0,c) and the second on [c,1), with f mapping [0,1) into itself. Given rational x and y in [0,1), does iterating f from x ever hit y exactly? Is there an algorithm that decides this for every such f, x, y? It is open even for two pieces, while in dimension two the analogous problem is undecidable.",
M=r"For $\theta=(a_1,b_1,a_2,b_2,c)\in\mathbb Q^5$ with $0<c<1$ let $f_\theta:[0,1)\to\mathbb R$, $f_\theta(t)=a_1t+b_1$ for $t<c$ and $f_\theta(t)=a_2t+b_2$ for $t\ge c$, and let $\Theta=\{\theta: f_\theta([0,1))\subseteq[0,1)\}$ (a decidable set). Let $\mathrm{Reach}_2=\{(\theta,x,y):\theta\in\Theta,\ x,y\in\mathbb Q\cap[0,1),\ \exists n\ge0\ f_\theta^{\,n}(x)=y\}$. Decide whether $\mathrm{Reach}_2$ is decidable (either answer, with proof, resolves the problem).",
why="Piecewise affine maps are the simplest hybrid dynamical systems; point-to-point reachability is undecidable for 2-dimensional PAMs (Koiran-Cosnard-Garzon 1994), while in dimension one it has been open for three decades, and Ghahremani-Kelmendi-Ouaknine call the two-interval case 'one of the most basic, longstanding open problems' in the area. Membership is recursively enumerable, so the question is whether non-reachability can always be certified; related variants are tied to Diophantine questions (Lagrange constants), as for the Positivity Problem for linear recurrences.",
issues="Corrected the setting to match the literature: the LICS 2023 paper (arXiv:2301.09752) works with self-maps of [0,1) with half-open intervals and rational coefficients, and proves decidability for INJECTIVE two-interval PAMs (the candidate's 'any number of pieces' was an overstatement). The candidate's version on all of Q is related but different: undecidability on [0,1) would imply undecidability on Q, but not conversely. I adopted the standard [0,1) version. Sanity checks: integer slopes give bounded denominators, so orbits are eventually periodic and the problem is decidable; one piece is decidable; the open content is non-injective maps with non-integer slopes. arXiv:2502.19923 (Bellman operators) and arXiv:2511.22979 (target discounted sum) are partial. No OpenAI title (affine/piecewise/reachab). Not a board duplicate (board 'affine prime maps' are a different, arithmetic object).",
refs=["https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.09752","https://people.mpi-sws.org/~joel/publications/pam23.pdf","https://livrepository.liverpool.ac.uk/3023232/","https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.19923","https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.04121"]))
